oor texture regions while breaking the\ntrain a classification CNN using a concatenation of the input image, noise vector, and\ndecoded image.\n2.2.3\nLocal patches investigation\nIn addition to global-level features, researchers are also exploring local features ob-\ntained from smaller patches. For instance, Jiaxuan Chen et al. [3] focus on the noise\npattern of a single patch with minimal texture diversity.\nThe core idea lies in the assumption that generative models often prioritise creating\npatches with detailed textures to enhance the realism of images while overlooking the\nsubtle noise introduced by the camera capture, especially in simple patches.\nTo improve robustness against image degradation, the patch goes through the\nEnhancement Module and is then classified after applying a high-pass filter.\nThe approach proposed by Yan Ju et al. [15] employs a two-branch model where\na Patch Selection Module extracts informative local patches, and an Attention-based\nFeature Fusion Module integrates spatial features from the entire image and local\nfeatures via multi-head attention. This enhances generalisation to unseen generative\nmodels, however, the result is still far from ideal.\nZhong et al. [50] introduce a detector that emphasizes texture-based fingerprints\nby exploiting the contrast between rich and poor texture regions while breaking the--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRegular Issue (https://www.ijimai.org/journal/sites/default/files/2024-11/ijimai_9_1_16.pdf)\nciteturn6search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.6 years ago; Xiao, T. ... Huang, “Emogen: Emotional image content generation ... Xue, G. ... Wang, L. ... Computer Science. https://cdn.openai.com/papers/dall-e- 3.pdf, vol. 2, no. ... synthetic image detection and model lineage analysis,” in Proceedings of ... Zhong, Y. ... Zhang, “Rich and poor texture contrast: A\nmedia images using frequency analysis,” in Disruptive Technologies in \nInformation Sciences VII, vol. 12542, 2023, pp. 44–56, SPIE.\n[76]\t Q. Bammey, “Synthbuster: Towards detection of diffusion model \ngenerated images,” IEEE Open Journal of Signal Processing, vol. 5, pp. 1–9, \n2023, doi: 10.1109/OJSP.2023.3337714.\n[77]\t T. Alzantot, C. Shou, M. Farag, Z. J. Wang, S. Pandey, M. Esmaili, \n“Wavelet-packets for deepfake image analysis and detection,” Machine \nLearning, vol. 111, no. 11, pp. 1–25, 2022, doi: 10.1007/s10994-022-06225-5.\n[78]\t N. Zhong, Y. Xu, Z. Qian, X. Zhang, “Rich and poor texture contrast: A \nsimple yet effective approach for ai-generated image detection,” arXiv \n- 203 ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ncakephp - Error: SQLSTATE[42P01]: Undefined table: \"Missing FROM-claouse entry for table - Stack Overflow (https://stackoverflow.com/questions/57504543/error-sqlstate42p01-undefined-table-missing-from-claouse-entry-for-table?noredirect=1)\nciteturn6search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.1 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago;                                     'dtreats.diagnosis_bind_id = dbinds.id'\nSo what's wrong in my `find` above. Thank you\n\nNote:\n\n  * I'm using cakephp 2.0\n\n  * Postgresql\n\n  * cakephp\n  * cakephp-2.0\n\nImprove this question\n\nFollow [Input]\n\nedited Aug 15, 2019 at 3:36\n\nasked Aug 15, 2019 at 3:30\n\nMaryadi Poipo\n\n1,478 9 9 gold badges 33 33 silver badges 55 55 bronze badges\n\n\n[Input]\n\n1\n\n[Input]\n\nThere are no `table` and `alias` options for query builder finders, they are only available for joins, so your main table will use the default alias as you can see in the query in the error message, and that alias is `DiagnosisBind`, hence using `dbinds` will cause an error.\n\nLong story short, use `DiagnosisBind` instead of `dbinds`.\n\nSide note, never ever inject data into single value conditions (or in the key of a `key => value` condition for that matter):\n    \n    `'dbinds.diagnosis_id ='.$diagnosisData[0][0]['id']\n    `\n\nThat's a possible SQL injection vulnerability!, always use the `key => value` syntax, or bindings:\n    \n    `'DiagnosisBind.diagnosis_id' => $diagnosisData[0][0]['id']\n    `\n\nImprove this answer\n\nFollow [Input]\n\nanswered Aug 15, 2019 at 21:21\n\nndm\n\n60.6k 9 9 gold badges 74 74 silver badges 113 113 bronze badges\n\nMaryadi Poipo Over a year ago\n\nThanks for your answer Sir.. One thing left is `'DiagnosisBind.diagnosis_id' => $diagnosisData[0][0]['id']`, if I use this way then diagnosis_id will be seen as string. That's why I'm using `=`--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDetection of AI generated images using rich and poor texture contrast | by Hriday Keswani | Medium (https://medium.com/%40hridaykeswani/detection-of-ai-generated-images-using-rich-and-poor-texture-contrast-fc2024e3e716)\nciteturn6search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.5 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; While searching for ways to detect AI generated images, I came across a novel research paper titled: “Rich and Poor Texture Contrast: A Simple yet Effective Approach for AI-generated Image Detection”.\n\n  1. Problem 1:\n  2. Solution to Problem 1\n  3. What is Smash&Reconstruction?\n  4. What are Rich and Poor texture patches?\n  5. How to get rich and poor texture patches of an image?\n  6. Problem 2:\n  7. Why did we separate patches by their texture?\n  8. How is the contrast between rich and poor texture patches of any help?\n  9. The contrast between these after applying 30 high pass filters on each image:\n  10. More about the high pass filters:\n  11. Links and references:\n\n# Detection of AI generated images using rich and poor texture contrast\n\nHriday Keswani\n\n6 min read\n\n·\n\nMar 3, 2024\n\n\nDeveloping a deep learning model to detect AI generated images\n\nWhile searching for ways to detect AI generated images, I came across a novel research paper titled: “Rich and Poor Texture Contrast: A Simple yet Effective Approach for AI-generated Image Detection”. Most Deep Learning approaches for detecting AI generated images depend on the method by which an image was generated or they depend on nature/semantics of the image where models can only detect AI generated humans, faces, cars or a particular object for which they were trained.\n\nThe proposed method overcomes the above mentioned issues and works for a much wider range of cases. I attempt to explain the paper and how it overcomes the problems faced by many other methods for detecting AI generated images. (link for implementation of the paper at the end)\n\n## Problem 1:\n\nWhen we train an existing model(like ResNet-50) to detect AI generated images, the model learns from the semantics of an image. This means if we train a model to detect AI generated images of car by using real images and AI generated images of different cars, the knowledge gained from that data cannot be applied for detection of AI generated images of humans.\n\nWhile we can train on data of a variety of objects, when we (my project group) tried doing so, it was a much slower approach and was able to give around 72% accuracy on unseen data. We can improve the accuracy by more training and having more data to train on, but we didn’t find that approach to be feasible.\n\n## Solution to Problem 1\n\nThe paper proposes a unique way to prevent the model from learning about AI generated features from an image’s shape (during training). It achieves this by a method named Smash&Reconstruction.\n\n## What is Smash&Reconstruction?\n\nIn this method we divide the image into patches of a predetermined sized and shuffle them to form a new image. This is a simpler explanation because there is one additional step before forming the final image.\n\nPress enter or click to view image in full size\n\nDivision of images in patches\n\nAfter division of images into patches we separate the patches in two groups, one group having patches with rich texture and the other group having patches with poor texture.\n\n## What are Rich and Poor texture patches?\n\nRegion of an image that is rich in details such as an object or borders between two contrasting color regions becomes a rich texture patch. Rich texture patches have high variation in pixels as compared to poor texture regions which are primarily background patches, such as the sky or still water.\n\n## How to get rich and poor texture patches of an image?\n\nWe first divide the image into patches of a predetermined size as shown earlier. Then we find the pixel gradient of these patches of the image (i.e. finding the difference between pixel values in horizontal, diagonal and counter diagonal directions and adding them all together) and separate them them into rich texture patches and poor texture patches.\n\nPatches having rich texture will have higher pixel gradient values when compared to poor texture patches. (This separation of patches partially helps solve problem 2, which is explained ahead)\n\nPress enter or click to view image in full size\n\nFormula to calculate pixel gradient of a patch\n\nAfter separating images on the basis of pixel contrast, we get two resultant images. This process is the complete process named as Smash&Reconstruction in the paper.\n\nResult of Smash&Reconstruction\n\nNote: Smash&Reconstruct may not work for overcoming problem 1 in other approaches of detecting AI generated images but it is beneficial for the complete approach proposed in the paper.\n\n## Problem 2:\n\nWe use a fingerprint approach, that is extracting details from the composition of an image instead of identifying abnormal patterns in an image, such as distorted hands of an AI generated human.\n\nMost fingerprint based approaches are limited by image generation techniques, which means that those models/algorithms can only detect images which were generated by particular method/similar methods such as Diffusion, GAN or other CNN based image generation methods.\n\nWe separated the patches as rich or poor texture to solve this problem precisely.\n\n## Why did we separate patches by their texture?\n\nThe authors propose a novel way of identifying fingerprints of AI generated images which is the paper’s title as well. They propose finding the contrast between rich and poor texture patches of the image after applying 30 high pass filters (as mentioned in the paper).\n\n## How is the contrast between rich and poor texture patches of any help?\n\nTo give a better example we compare to images side by side, a real image and an AI generated image.\n\nComparing an AI generated image and a real image side by side\n\nPutting each of them through Smash&Reconstruction process:\n\nReal Image results after Smash&Reconstruct process\n\nPress enter or click to view image in full size\n\nAI image results after Smash&Reconstruct process\n\n## The contrast between these after applying 30 high pass filters on each image:\n\nEven the poor texture patches of a real image have consistent features\n\nPress enter or click to view image in full size\n\nThere is lower consistency of features in the poor texture patches of an AI generated image\n\nAs we can observe in these results, the AI generated image has much higher contrast between rich and poor texture patches when compared with real image’s contrast.\n\nAfter these steps are complete, we put the results through a trainable model and feed the resultant data into a classifier as shown in the diagram below.\n\nPress enter or click to view image in full size\n\narchitecture of the complete method\n\nThe 30 high pass filters are mentioned in the paper, these filters were originally introduced for steganalysis..\n\nThe structure of the classifier is mentioned below:\n\nPress enter or click to view image in full size\n\nscreenshot of the structure of the classifier\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n使用纹理对比度检测检测AI生成的图像-阿里云开发者社区 (https://developer.aliyun.com/article/1450469)\nciteturn6search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.5 years ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; 但是这篇论文“Rich and Poor Texture Contrast: A Simple yet Effective Approach for AI-generated Image Detection”所提出的方法克服了上述问题，适用范围更广。 ... 广义上，只要通过某种方式将信息隐藏到图片中而难以通过普通方式发现，就可以称为图片隐写，对于隐写分析有很多相关的研究，有兴趣的可以查阅相关资料。\n\n但是这篇论文“Rich and Poor Texture Contrast: A Simple yet Effective Approach for AI-generated Image Detection”所提出的方法克服了上述问题，适用范围更广。我们将解释这篇论文，以及它是如何解决许多其他检测人工智能生成图像的方法所面临的问题的。\n\n## 泛化性问题\n\n当我们训练一个模型(如ResNet-50)来检测人工智能生成的图像时，模型会从图像的语义中学习。如果训练一个通过使用真实图像和人工智能生成的不同汽车图像来检测人工智能生成的汽车图像的模型，那么目前的模型只能从该数据中获得有关汽车的信息，而对于其他的物体就无法进行判别\n\n虽然可以在各种对象的数据上进行训练，但当我们尝试这样做时，这种方法慢得多，并且只能够在未见过的数据上给出大约72%的准确率。虽然可以通过更多的训练和更多的数据来提高准确率，但我们不可能找到无穷无尽的数据进行训练。\n他们提出在应用30个高通滤波器后，找到图像中丰富和贫乏纹理斑块之间的对比度。\n\n丰富和贫乏的纹理块之间的对比度有什么帮助呢？\n\n为了更好理解，我们将图像并排比较，真实图像和人工智能生成的图像。\n\nImage\n\n这两张图像使用肉眼观看也是很难查看他们的去别的对吧\n\n论文首先使用Smash&Reconstruction 过程:\n\nImage\n\nImage\n\n在每个图像上应用30个高通滤波器后，它们之间的对比度:\nImage\n\nImage\n\n从这些结果中我们可以看到，人工智能生成的图像与真实图像的对比度相比，纹理斑块丰富和贫乏的对比度要高得多。\n\n这样我们用肉眼就可以看到区别了，所以可以将对比度的结果放入可训练模型，并将结果数据输入分类器，这样就是我们这篇论文的模型架构：\n\nImage\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nClass HaskellParser.BindsContext (https://kevingliewe.github.io/GCore.Antlr.Grammers/api/GCore.Antlr.Grammers.Haskell.HaskellParser.BindsContext.html)\nciteturn6search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4.7 years ago; #### dbinds()\n\n#### Accept<TResult>(IParseTreeVisitor<TResult>)\n\n##### Declaration\n    \n    `public override TResult Accept<TResult>(IParseTreeVisitor<TResult> visitor)`\n\n##### Parameters\n\nType  | Name  | Description\n--- | --- | ---\nIParseTreeVisitor<TResult>  | visitor  | \n\n##### Returns\n\nType  | Description\n--- | ---\nTResult  | \n\n##### Type Parameters\n\nName  | Description\n--- | ---\nTResult  | \n| Improve this Doc View Source\n\n#### close()\n\n##### Declaration\n    \n    `public HaskellParser.CloseContext close()`\n\n##### Returns\n\nType  | Description\n--- | ---\nHaskellParser.CloseContext  | \n| Impr